WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.950
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.480
What are datasets?

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.280
A dataset is typically a big bunch of data,
for instance, a database of written letters,

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:18.090
digits, images of human faces, stock market
data that scientists can use to test their

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.410
algorithms on.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:25.030
If two research groups wish to find out whose
algorithm performs better at recognizing traffic

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:29.640
signs, they run their techniques on one of
these datasets and test their methods on equal

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.860
footings.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:36.750
For instance, the CamVid dataset stands for
Cambridge-driving Labeled Video Database,

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:41.520
and it offers several hundreds of images depicting
a variety of driving scenarios.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.170
It is meant to be used to test classification
techniques: the input is an image, and the

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:52.350
question is for each of the pixels, which
one of them belongs to what class.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:58.790
Classes include roads, vegetation, vehicles,
pedestrians, buildings, trees and more.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:03.059
These regions are labeled with all the different
colors that you see on these images.

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:08.320
To have a usable dataset, we have to label
tens of thousands of these images, and as

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.710
you may imagine, creating such labeled images
requires a ton of human labor.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.490
The first guy has to accurately trace the
edges of each of the individual objects seen

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.209
on every image, and there should be a second
guy to cross-check and make sure everything

00:01:23.209 --> 00:01:24.399
is in order.

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:25.500
That's quite a chore.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.920
And we haven't even talked about all the other
problems that arise from processing footage

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.619
created with handheld cameras, so this takes
quite a bit of time and effort with stabilization

00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:37.179
and calibration as well.

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:43.069
So how do we create huge and accurate datasets
without investing a remarkable amount of human

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.069
labor?

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:46.670
Well, hear out this incredible idea.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:52.319
What if we would record a video of us wandering
about in an open-world computer game, and

00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:54.219
annotate those images.

00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:57.789
This way, we enjoy several advantages:
- 1.

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:02.729
Since we have recorded continuous videos,
after annotating the very first image, we

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:07.630
will have information from the next frames,
therefore if we do it well, we can propagate

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.300
a labeling from one image to the next one.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.110
That's a huge time saver.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.110
- 2.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:18.610
In a computer game, one can stage and record
animations of important, but rare situations

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.560
that would otherwise be extremely difficult
to film.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.750
Adding rain or day and night cycles to a set
of images is also trivial, because we simply

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.480
can query the game engine to do this for us.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.480
- 3.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.960
Not only that, but the algorithm also has
some knowledge about the rendering process

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:35.960
itself.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:41.000
This means that it looks at how the game communicates
with the software drivers and the video card,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.090
tracks when the geometry and textures for
a given type of car are being loaded or discarded,

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.840
and uses this information to further help
the label propagation process.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.840
- 4.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.940
We don't have any of the problems that stem
from using handheld cameras.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:02.770
Noise, blurriness, problems with the lens,
and so on are all non-issues.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:08.790
Using this previous CamVid dataset, the annotation
of one image takes around 60 minutes, while

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.540
with this dataset, 7 seconds.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:17.400
Thus, the authors have published almost 25000
high-quality images and their annotations

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.440
to aid computer vision and machine learning
research in the future.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.850
That's a lot of images, but of course, the
ultimate question arises: how do we know if

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:29.819
these are really high-quality training samples?

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:32.420
They were only taken from a computer game
after all!

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:38.380
Well, the results show that using this dataset,
we can achieve an equivalent quality of learning

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.900
compared to the CamVid dataset by using one
third as many images.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.830
Excellent piece of work, absolutely loving
the idea of using video game footage as a

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.360
surrogate for real-world data.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.360
Fantastic.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.630
And in the meantime, while we're discussing
computer graphics, here's a nice computer

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.950
graphics challenge grant from Experiment.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.670
Basically, if you start a new research project
through their crowdfunded system, you may

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.210
win additional funding that comes straight
for them.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.210
Free money.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:12.420
If you are interested in doing any kind of
research in this area, or if you are a long-time

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.480
practitioner, make sure to have a look.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.079
The link is available in the video description
box.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:21.399
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

